# Chroma library, modern Earth: RETURNS for Picks come back (10-17), set b: kept and broke your word, wild risk, turned down a chance, took them in, kept it hidden.
# Played only with the engine's echo_play switch on. Syntax: header of build.py. Each return: W U B R G once each and one full cross cycle (s1 to s4 in turn).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- of: kept your word

== the friend you never let down asks you to hold everything
tier: return
of: kept your word
side: well
stages: adult+
age: 30-110
stakes: .7
tone: mixed
life: friends, family, money
horizon: years
roles: friend, a lawyer, the friend's relatives
earth: an old friend you always kept faith with asks you to be named in their will, and to speak for them if they fall ill
delay: 5-30
likelier: the old promise was kept at a real cost; the friend has little close family; both are past middle age
rarer: the friend has a large family close by; the two have drifted apart
times: Fairly common: many people name a trusted friend rather than a relative to act for them; perhaps 1 in 5 who kept a long promise to a friend are later asked (estimate)
scene: {friend} pushes a folder across the kitchen table. Inside are a will and a form for speaking on {friend}'s behalf in hospital. There is a blank where {Ns} name should go.
scene W: {N} kept {Ns} word to {friend} once, when it was hard. Now {friend} asks for the heaviest trust there is: to act for {friend} when {friend} no longer can.
scene U: {N} reads the papers slowly while {friend} watches. Accounts, a flat, a dog, the hospital forms. It is a lot to understand, and a lot to get right.
scene B: Being named by {friend} brings a say over money, a home and hard choices. Some of {friend}'s relatives will not like that at all.
scene R: {N} has to look away for a moment. {friend} is saying, plainly, that {N} is the one person in the world {friend} trusts most.
scene G: Trust grows slowly, like an orchard. {N} planted it years ago by keeping a promise to {friend}, and here is the fruit, heavy on the branch.
scene gone: {friend} is not here to push the folder across the table. Yet {Ns} name sits in {friend}'s papers all the same, put there because of a promise kept.
- W | sign, and promise to carry out every wish exactly as written | binds | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 76
- U | go through every paper together first, and ask what each wish really means | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 78
- B | agree, on condition that the costs of the work come out of the estate | v: power, security | chance: 80
- R | say yes at once, and reach across the table to hug them | v: benevolence | chance: 89
- G | suggest they name a relative beside you, so the weight is shared as families share it | v: tradition, security | chance: 74
- W>B | accept, and insist on a proper lawyer so nobody can challenge it later | v: security, power | chance: 78
- U>R | ask what they most want from the years ahead, and plan a trip around it | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 70
- B>G | agree, and ask to be left the old family house in return | v: power, tradition | chance: 55
- R>W | sign tonight, and go and tell their relatives yourself, face to face | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 60
- G>U | ask for a season to think it through before giving any answer | v: self-direction, security | chance: 65
works: {friend} signs the papers with a steady hand, and something settles between them.
works not B>G: The trust holds, and the years that follow feel easier for both of them.
fails: {friend}'s relatives hear of it and make a family quarrel of it.
fails: The papers turn out tangled, and sorting them out takes months.

== a costly promise asks to be made again
tier: return
of: kept your word
side: badly
stages: young_adult+
age: 22-110
stakes: .7
tone: trouble
life: family, money
horizon: months
roles: sibling, parent, a bank
earth: you kept a promise to cover a brother or sister once, and it cost you dearly; now they ask for the same promise again
delay: 2-20
likelier: the first cost was large and never spoken of; the sibling is hopeful by nature; the person has savings again
rarer: the sibling repaid or thanked the person properly; the two rarely speak
times: Common: perhaps 1 in 3 people who once covered a relative's loss are asked to do it again (estimate)
scene: {sibling} rings, sounding just like last time. {N} once promised to cover {sibling} if the business failed. It failed, and {N} paid without a word. Now there is a new plan.
scene W: {N} kept {Ns} word to {sibling} last time, to the last of it. A promise is a promise. But nobody ever said it had to be made twice.
scene U: {N} adds up what the old promise to {sibling} cost: the savings, a move put off, two lean years. {sibling} has never once asked about that.
scene B: Last time {N} carried the risk for {sibling} and got nothing for it. If {sibling} wants that again, it should come with terms.
scene R: {N} hears the hopeful voice of {sibling} and feels the old knot of love and resentment pull tight at once.
scene G: Some family patterns run deep as roots. {sibling} leans and {N} holds, and it has always been that way between them.
scene gone: {sibling} is not here to ask again. Still, when someone else needs a guarantor, {N} remembers keeping that promise to {sibling}, and what it cost.
- W | say no clearly, and explain that the old promise was enough | v: security, conformity | chance: 66
- U | ask to see the whole plan, and promise nothing until it adds up | v: self-direction, security | chance: 76
- B | agree only for a written share in the new business | v: power, achievement | chance: 77
- R | tell them at last how much the last promise hurt | v: none clear (an old hurt said out loud) | chance: 66
- G | promise again; family carries family, whatever it costs | binds | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 59
- W>R | offer a smaller promise you can truly keep, and say why with warmth | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 80
- U>G | help them find a bank or backer, so the risk sits where it belongs | v: security, benevolence | chance: 70
- B>W | ask them to repay the old loss first, a little each month, as is fair | v: universalism (fairness), power | chance: 60
- R>U | spend a day out together, and talk the old years through honestly | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 75
- G>B | keep the peace at home, but quietly move your savings out of reach | v: security, tradition | chance: 76
works: {sibling} takes it better than {N} feared, and the old knot loosens.
works: {N} and {sibling} find terms that leave both of them standing.
fails: {sibling} goes quiet, and the next family dinner is very cold.
fails: Old resentment surfaces, and the call ends worse than it began.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- of: broke your word

== now it is their turn to back out
tier: return
of: broke your word
side: well
stages: juvenile+
age: 16-110
stakes: .6
tone: mixed
life: friends, plans
horizon: week
roles: friend, partner
earth: years after a friend forgave you for backing out of a promise, they have to back out of one made to you
delay: 1-20
likelier: the old forgiveness was generous and remembered; a shared plan with money already spent
rarer: the two now see each other seldom; nothing big was planned together
times: Common: most close friendships see each side back out of something at least once (estimate)
scene: {friend} calls the week before the trip they planned together. Something has come up, and {friend} cannot go. Years ago it was {N} who made that same call.
scene W: {friend} forgave {N} for breaking {Ns} word back then. Fair is fair. But {N} took leave, paid the deposit and planned for months.
scene U: {N} tries to see it from {friend}'s side, as it was all those years ago, and wonders whether the reason now is as good as {Ns} was then.
scene B: {friend} owes {N} for that old grace, and both of them know it. Now {friend} needs the same grace back.
scene R: The letdown lands hard. Then comes the memory of {friend}'s voice, years ago, saying never mind, it happens.
scene G: The wheel turns slowly. Once {N} let {friend} down and was let off. Now the turn has come round to {friend}.
scene gone: {friend} is not here to call and cancel. Still, when someone backs out of a plan, {N} remembers how {friend} once let {N} off without a word of blame.
- W | let them off as freely as they once did, with no questions | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 80
- U | ask what has come up, and whether part of the plan can still be saved | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 73
- B | ask them to cover the deposit, since the plan was half theirs | v: power, security | chance: 79
- R | tell them it stings, and forgive them in the same breath | v: benevolence | chance: 83
- G | shrug it off; plans change, and friendships outlast them | v: tradition (accepting what is) | chance: 89
- W>G | go on the trip alone, and send them a card from every stop | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 78
- U>W | agree on a new date together, written down, with a fair way to cancel | v: conformity, security | chance: 70
- B>U | sell their place on the trip, and see what the new company teaches | v: power, self-direction | chance: 66
- R>B | take someone new along, and make sure they hear how good it was | v: hedonism, power | chance: 72
- G>R | drop everything and go to them, in case the something is serious | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 80
works: The old grace is paid forward, and {friend} says nobody else would have been so kind.
works: The plan changes shape, and turns out better than the first one.
fails: Something in {N} keeps score after all, and the friendship cools a little.
fails: The new plans fall through too, and both are left out of pocket.

== a cool invitation from the one you let down
tier: return
of: broke your word
side: badly
stages: juvenile+
age: 16-110
stakes: .8
tone: trouble
life: family, friends
horizon: months
roles: sibling, cousins, an aunt
earth: the brother or sister you once let down invites you to their wedding, politely, as if expecting you not to come
delay: 2-25
likelier: the broken promise was about being there on a big day; the family is close-knit; nobody spoke of it after
rarer: the person apologised at the time; the families live far apart
times: Fairly common: perhaps 1 in 4 people who let a sibling down on a big day get a second big day to turn up for (estimate)
scene: The card from {sibling} is polite and short: a wedding, a date, a place. No note. The last time {N} promised to be there for {sibling}, {N} was not.
scene W: {N} broke {Ns} word to {sibling} once. {sibling} never complained, and never quite trusted {N} again either.
scene U: {N} reads the card from {sibling} three times, looking for warmth between the lines and finding only good manners.
scene B: Turning up for {sibling} now could win back a place in the family. Staying away would close that door for good.
scene R: {Ns} throat tightens. {sibling} sent the card at all, which means {sibling} still hopes, a little.
scene G: Old wounds heal from the edges inward. {sibling} has left a gap just wide enough for {N} to step through.
scene gone: {sibling} is not here to send a card. Still, an invitation to someone else's wedding brings back the day {N} did not turn up for {sibling}.
- W | say yes in writing, and arrive early, whatever happens | binds | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: kept your word | chance: 81
- U | call first, and ask plainly whether the invitation is meant or only polite | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 68
- B | send the biggest gift on the list, and let it speak for itself | v: power, conformity | chance: 65
- R | go round and say sorry properly, before the day itself | v: benevolence | mark: owned up | chance: 79
- G | reply kindly but stay away, and let them have the day without old shadows | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 64
- W>U | offer to take charge of one part of the day, and see it through | v: benevolence, achievement | chance: 72
- U>B | find out who else is going, and which old friends could ease the way | v: security, power | chance: 72
- B>R | turn up with a hired band and set the whole party dancing | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 50
- R>G | write a long letter about the old days, and post it before the wedding | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 75
- G>W | ask an aunt or cousin to tell them you will come, and mean it this time | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 70
works: {sibling} reads {Ns} answer, and some of the old ease comes back.
works not G: The day goes well, and {sibling} squeezes {Ns} hand at the end of it.
fails: {sibling} stays polite, and nothing more.
fails not G: Old talk starts up among the relatives, and the day is spoiled for {N}.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- of: took a wild risk

== their child wants to do what you did
tier: return
of: took a wild risk
side: well
stages: adult+
age: 35-110
stakes: .7
tone: mixed
life: friends, family, risk
horizon: months
roles: friend, the friend's grown child
earth: the friend who shared your wildest adventure asks you to talk their own grown child out of trying the same thing
delay: 15-35
likelier: the adventure is a family legend; the friend's child admires the person; the two friends stayed close
rarer: the adventure was kept quiet; the friend's children are still small
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 8 people who took a famous risk with a friend are later asked to talk that friend's child out of it (estimate)
scene: {friend} calls late. {friend}'s eldest, nineteen now, wants to cross the mountains by motorbike, as {N} and {friend} once did. Would {N} talk some sense into the young one?
scene W: {N} knows the trip went well for {N} and {friend} by luck as much as skill. A grown-up's job is to say so plainly.
scene U: {N} thinks back over the trip with {friend}: what they planned, what they got wrong, and what saved them twice.
scene B: {friend} wants {N} on side. The young one looks up to {N}, so whatever {N} says will carry weight in {friend}'s house.
scene R: {N} grins before {friend} has finished. It was the best summer of {Ns} life, and talking anyone out of it feels like a lie.
scene G: Every generation finds its own edge. {friend} and {N} found theirs, and now the next one is looking for its own.
scene gone: {friend} is not here to ask. But one day {friend}'s eldest turns up with a map and the old photographs, wanting the whole story from {N}.
- W | tell the young one the honest risks, and back the parent's decision | v: conformity, security | chance: 74
- U | sit down with them over maps, and plan a safer route together | v: self-direction, security | chance: 78
- B | offer to pay for proper training, on condition they wait a year | v: power, security | chance: 80
- R | tell them every story, the good and the frightening, and let them choose | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 71
- G | leave it to them; the young find their own edge either way | v: tradition (accepting what is) | chance: 69
- W>B | lend them the old gear, but only with a written plan for checking in | v: security, achievement | chance: 75
- U>R | ride the first stretch with them, and see how they handle the road | v: stimulation, security | chance: 62
- B>G | find them a season of paid work on a mountain farm instead | v: achievement, universalism (nature) | chance: 66
- R>W | make it a shared trip: the two old friends and the young one together | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 55
- G>U | suggest they start smaller, on the hills nearby, and learn their way up | v: self-direction, security | chance: 78
works: The young one sets out wiser, and comes back with stories of their own.
works not R: {friend} thanks {N}, and the two old friends feel as close as ever.
fails: The young one goes anyway, against all advice, and the parents lose sleep for weeks.
fails not W: {friend} feels {N} took the young one's side, and things are stiff between them.

== the one who lost with you wants another go
tier: return
of: took a wild risk
side: badly
stages: young_adult+
age: 22-110
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: work, money, friends
horizon: months
roles: friend, partner, colleague
earth: the friend who lost money and years with you in a wild venture that failed is back, steadier now, asking to try again properly
delay: 3-20
likelier: the two stayed in touch after the failure; the friend has rebuilt; the person has money again
rarer: the failure ended in a quarrel; the person has heavy duties now
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 8 people whose venture with a friend failed are later asked by that friend to try again (estimate)
scene: {friend} sits down across from {N} in the old cafe. Their wild venture together sank years ago and took both their savings. Now {friend} has a new plan, and wants {N} in.
scene W: {N} still feels a debt to {friend} for that failure. {N} pushed hardest back then, and {friend} lost the most.
scene U: {friend}'s new plan is careful in all the places the old one was reckless. {N} reads it twice, looking for the old mistakes.
scene B: {N} has more to lose now than then. If {N} joins {friend} this time, the terms will have to show it.
scene R: Seeing {friend} light up over a plan again brings back the whole wild year, the hope and the crash both.
scene G: A field burned once can grow back richer. {friend} has spent years letting the ground recover. Perhaps it is ready.
scene gone: {friend} is not here to bring a new plan. But when a chance like the old one comes, {N} remembers the wild year with {friend}, and how it ended.
- W | say no, but offer to repay a fair part of the old loss | v: universalism (fairness), conformity | chance: 66
- U | ask for every number, and test the plan against what went wrong before | v: self-direction, security | chance: 76
- B | join only under a contract that caps what can be lost | v: security, power | chance: 81
- R | say yes on the spot; some dreams deserve a second try | v: stimulation, benevolence | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 46
- G | wish them well, and watch from the side this time | v: security, tradition (accepting what is) | chance: 84
- W>R | promise to help on evenings and weekends, for the friendship, not the money | v: benevolence | chance: 75
- U>G | join slowly: one small step, then another only if the first one holds | v: security, self-direction | chance: 72
- B>W | bring in a lawyer to set fair shares before anything is spent | v: security, universalism | chance: 74
- R>U | spend a weekend hearing how they rebuilt, before deciding anything | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 82
- G>B | put in only what you can bear to lose, and not a crumb more | v: security, power | chance: 76
works: The second try is steadier than the first, and the old loss stops stinging.
works: {N} and {friend} talk it through honestly, and the old failure is finally laid down.
fails not G: The old hopes come rushing back, and so does the old trouble.
fails: {friend} hears caution as blame, and leaves the cafe hurt.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- of: turned down a chance

== the one who said yes in your place
tier: return
of: turned down a chance
side: well
stages: young_adult+
age: 25-110
stakes: .7
tone: mixed
life: work, friends
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss
earth: the colleague who took the post you turned down years ago is worn out by it, and comes to you to talk
delay: 3-20
likelier: a small field where people stay in touch; a demanding post; the person has done well on another road
rarer: the post went to a stranger; the person left the field entirely
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 6 people who turned down a post later hear from the one who took it (estimate)
scene: {colleague} took the post {N} turned down years ago. {N} has done well since, on another road. Tonight {colleague} is at {Ns} door, worn thin, wanting to talk.
scene W: {N} said no back then, and {colleague} said yes and has carried the load ever since. {N} feels a quiet duty to hear {colleague} out.
scene U: {N} listens to {colleague} describe the job and recognises every reason {N} turned it down. The question is what {colleague} should do now.
scene B: {colleague} sits in the seat {N} could have had. If {colleague} leaves it, that seat may come free again, and with it a lot of say.
scene R: {N} sees how tired {colleague} looks and feels a jolt of guilt, as if the tiredness were partly {Ns} doing.
scene G: Each life grows where it is planted. {N} grew one way and {colleague} another, and neither knew then which ground would suit.
scene gone: {colleague} is not here to talk it over. Still, {N} hears of someone worn thin by that same post, and thinks of {colleague}, who said yes in {Ns} place.
- W | hear them out fully, and keep what they say strictly between the two of you | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 84
- U | help them list what they would keep in the job and what they would drop | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 78
- B | ask quietly whether the post might come free, and who would decide | v: power, achievement | chance: 75
- R | tell them straight: leave, because life is too short for this | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 61
- G | tell them why you said no back then, and let them draw their own lesson | v: self-direction, tradition | chance: 79
- W>G | urge them to take the long leave the rules allow, and rest | v: security, benevolence | chance: 72
- U>W | help them draft a fair plan to share the load with others at work | v: universalism, achievement | chance: 70
- B>U | introduce them to people on your own road who might hire them | v: achievement, benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 70
- R>B | offer to step into the post for a while, so they can breathe | v: benevolence, power | chance: 55
- G>R | take them walking in the hills for a weekend, phones off | v: hedonism, universalism (nature) | chance: 82
works: {colleague} leaves with a clearer head and a plan to go on with.
works: The old choice looks right for both of them now, and they part as friends.
fails: {colleague} hears only envy or pity in what {N} says, and goes cold.
fails: Nothing changes for {colleague}, and {N} carries a strange guilt about it.

