horizon: week
roles: friend
earth: the friend who once lived with you and left on bad terms sends back your old house key with a letter, asking to meet
delay: 2-20
likelier: the quarrel was over small things that grew; the friend has settled since; mutual friends still in touch
rarer: real harm was done; the two have no friends in common
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 6 people whose lodger left on bad terms hear from them again years later (estimate)
scene: A small parcel arrives. Inside is the old house key {friend} never gave back, and a letter. {friend} lived with {N} for a year once, and it ended with a slammed door.
scene W: {friend} left owing {N} rent and an apology. The letter from {friend} says it is time to settle both.
scene U: {N} reads {friend}'s letter carefully. It is honest about some things and quiet about others, and {N} wants to know which is which.
scene B: {friend} wants something from {N}, or there would be no letter. Forgiveness, perhaps, or a favour. Either way, the choice sits with {N}.
scene R: {N} turns the key over in {Ns} hand, and the whole year with {friend} floods back: the laughter at first, the shouting at the end.
scene G: Some things take time to find their way home. {friend} has taken years, but the key is back where it belongs.
scene gone: {friend} is not here to post a letter. But the old key turns up in a drawer, and {N} thinks of the year {friend} lived here, and the slammed door.
- W | write back, and agree to meet somewhere neither of you calls home | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 78
- U | reply briefly, asking what exactly they want to say | v: self-direction, security | chance: 78
- B | answer that the old rent comes first, and the talking after | v: power, security | chance: 67
- R | ring them that night, before second thoughts can win | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 76
- G | keep the key, leave the letter unanswered, and let the past rest | v: tradition (accepting what is), security | chance: 79
- W>R | meet them, and say how that year felt, kindly but fully | v: benevolence | chance: 68
- U>G | ask a friend you both know how they have been, before deciding | v: security, tradition | chance: 78
- B>W | let them repay the rent in small parts, written down and fair to both | v: universalism (fairness), security | chance: 72
- R>U | invite them round for dinner, and talk it out at the old table | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 66
- G>B | answer warmly, but never offer the spare room again | v: security, benevolence | chance: 80
works not G: {N} and {friend} talk, and the slammed door finally swings open again.
works: The old year settles into something {N} can remember without a wince.
fails not G G>B: The meeting circles the old quarrel, and nothing is mended.
fails: {friend} reads {Ns} answer, or silence, as a no, and the distance comes back.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- of: kept it hidden (who they love or their gender; the choice and the trust, never a verdict)

== the one who kept your confidence asks you to keep theirs
tier: return
of: kept it hidden
side: well
stages: young_adult+
age: 20-110
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: friends, inner, trust
horizon: months
roles: friend
earth: the one friend who knew your private truth and never told anyone now has something private of their own, and brings it to you
delay: 3-30
likelier: a long friendship; the friend kept the confidence through hard times; the friend has few others to turn to
rarer: the two have drifted apart; the friend has a wide circle close by
times: Fairly common: most people who keep something private about who they love or who they are share it with one or two trusted people, and those ties tend to run both ways (estimate)
scene: For years {friend} was the one person who knew, and never let it slip. Tonight {friend} sits on {Ns} sofa, turning a cup, and says there is something {friend} has told nobody.
scene W: {friend} kept {Ns} confidence through every family party and awkward question. A confidence kept like that is a debt of honour, and {N} means to honour it.
scene U: {N} knows how much it takes to say a private thing aloud for the first time. {N} listens to {friend} closely, without rushing to understand.
scene B: Trust is the most precious thing one person can hold for another. {friend} gave it to {N} once, and now asks {N} to hold some in return.
scene R: {N} sees {friend}'s hands shaking and feels a rush of tenderness, remembering exactly how it felt to sit where {friend} sits now.
scene G: Some things grow best in a sheltered place. {N} once found shelter with {friend}. Now {friend} is looking for the same.
scene gone: {friend} is not here to talk to. But some evenings {N} remembers that {friend} knew, and kept it safe, and that it made all the difference.
- W | promise to keep it, as faithfully as they kept the old secret | binds | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 86
- U | listen without judging, then ask what kind of help they want | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 83
- B | help them weigh who, if anyone, ever needs to know | v: self-direction, security | chance: 83
- R | hug them, and tell them it changes nothing between the two of you | v: benevolence | chance: 89
- G | let them tell it at their own pace, over as many evenings as it takes | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 89
- W>G | offer your home as a place where they can always be fully themselves | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 82
- U>W | tell them how you carried your own private thing, if they want to hear | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 80
- B>U | help them find others who have faced the same, quietly and safely | v: security, self-direction | chance: 72
- R>B | swear on the spot to guard it as fiercely as your own | v: benevolence, security | chance: 85
- G>R | take them somewhere quiet and open, where breathing and talking come easier | v: universalism (nature), benevolence | chance: 82
works: {friend} breathes out, and something heavy turns into something shared.
works: The friendship deepens, and both of them carry their private things more lightly.
fails: {friend} takes it back, says it was nothing, and goes quiet for a while.
fails: The talk stirs up old worries for {N} too, and the night ends uneasy for both.

== someone young brings you the same weight
tier: return
of: kept it hidden
side: badly
stages: adult+
age: 30-110
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: family, inner, trust
horizon: months
roles: sibling, the sibling's teenager
earth: the brother or sister who nearly learned your private truth years ago has a teenager who now confides something private in you, and asks you not to tell anyone yet
delay: 10-35
likelier: the keeping was long and close-run; the person is the relative the young one feels easiest with; family meals bring them together often
rarer: the person lives far from the family; the sibling has no children of teenage years
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 10 people who kept something private about who they love or who they are later become the trusted grown-up for a young relative (estimate)
scene: Years ago {sibling} almost found out, and keeping it took all {N} had. Now {sibling}'s teenager follows {N} into the garden and, very quietly, shares something nobody at home knows.
scene W: {N} knows what it costs to keep something like this from {sibling}. Now a young one asks {N} to keep it too, and a promise to a young person weighs heavy.
scene U: {N} remembers every near miss with {sibling}: the letter, the questions, the long silences after. {N} wants to help {sibling}'s youngest without repeating any of it.
scene B: {N} is now the only grown-up who knows. That puts {N} in a careful spot between {sibling} and the young one, and the young one has to come first.
scene R: The young one has the same watchful look {N} wore for years at {sibling}'s table, and {Ns} heart goes out at once.
scene G: Some things come round again in a family, like a tree putting out the same leaf. {sibling}'s youngest is finding a path {N} once walked alone.
scene gone: {sibling} is not here any more. But one day {sibling}'s youngest seeks {N} out with something private, and {N} remembers how hard keeping things from {sibling} once was.
- W | promise to keep their confidence, for as long as they want it kept | binds | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 81
- U | listen, and ask what they need now, not what should come next | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 83
- B | help them think through who is safe to tell, and when, if ever | v: security, self-direction | chance: 85
- R | tell them they are fine just as they are, and mean every word | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 91
- G | let them know there is no hurry, and that every path is theirs to choose | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 89
- W>U | find a trusted counsellor or group for them, through proper channels | v: security, benevolence | chance: 75
- U>B | share how you managed it, the hard parts too, as much as you choose | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 78
- B>R | give them your number, and say they can call at any hour | v: benevolence, security | chance: 88
- R>G | take them on long walks, where talking side by side comes easy | v: benevolence, universalism (nature) | chance: 85
- G>W | keep the family calm, and be the steady grown-up at every gathering | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 80
works: The young one walks back from the garden lighter, knowing one grown-up understands.
works: Over the months {N} and {sibling}'s youngest grow close, and nobody has to carry it alone.
fails: {sibling} notices the quiet talks and starts asking questions, and {N} has to tread carefully.
fails: The old near misses come back to {N} at night, and the year is hard to carry.
